# ── OZ-POS Reverse Proxy + TLS (Caddy) — example configuration ────────
#
# The OZ-POS services speak plain HTTP; TLS is terminated by a reverse
# proxy in front of the stack. Caddy is recommended because it obtains
# and renews Let's Encrypt certificates automatically for public domains.
#
#   https://api.example.com      ──► pos-cloud-server :3099
#   https://license.example.com  ──► license-server   :8080
#
# Usage:
#   1. Replace `example.com` with your real domains.
#   2. Run Caddy with this file (see docs/operations/docker-deployment.md
#      § "Reverse Proxy & TLS (Caddy)" for the full setup).
#   3. When Caddy runs INSIDE the Compose network, upstreams resolve by
#      service name (pos-cloud-server:3099). When it runs on the host,
#      use 127.0.0.1:3099 / 127.0.0.1:8080 instead.
#
# Private / non-public deployment (no Let's Encrypt reachable DNS):
#   replace the site address with `:443` and add `tls internal` to use
#   Caddy's locally-issued CA. Clients must trust that CA.

# includeSubDomains is aggressive — drop it if this domain hosts unrelated
# subdomains (e.g. a marketing site) that must stay reachable over HTTP.
api.example.com {
	reverse_proxy pos-cloud-server:3099
	encode gzip
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		X-Frame-Options DENY
		Referrer-Policy no-referrer
	}
}

license.example.com {
	reverse_proxy license-server:8080
	encode gzip
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		X-Frame-Options DENY
		Referrer-Policy no-referrer
	}
}
